WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:03.213
- Pronouns' main purpose
is to replace nouns

00:00:03.213 --> 00:00:05.430
so that we don't have
to repeat the same words

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:08.252
or phrases over and over and over again.

00:00:08.252 --> 00:00:10.466
It's really a matter of convenience.

00:00:10.466 --> 00:00:12.387
Imagine if you had to say,

00:00:12.387 --> 00:00:14.577
"Did you tell Archibald
that Archibald's mother

00:00:14.577 --> 00:00:15.734
"is looking for Archibald?"

00:00:15.734 --> 00:00:18.380
And while there are lots of
different kinds of pronouns,

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:20.513
there's one type in particular

00:00:20.513 --> 00:00:22.920
that people tend to care about the most:

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:24.371
the personal pronoun,

00:00:24.371 --> 00:00:26.844
the short little words
we use as a substitute

00:00:26.844 --> 00:00:28.490
for a person's name.

00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:30.797
For example, "Where did Divya go?"

00:00:30.797 --> 00:00:32.226
"She went to the store."

00:00:32.226 --> 00:00:34.660
Now you may have noticed that "they"

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:37.555
is both singular and
plural in our chart here.

00:00:37.555 --> 00:00:40.980
That's one of the big fusses
about pronouns these days.

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:44.807
But I'm here to tell you that
singular they is nothing new.

00:00:44.807 --> 00:00:46.440
The Oxford English Dictionary

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:49.690
traces it all the way back to 1375.

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:52.034
And that's just the first
time it was written down.

00:00:52.034 --> 00:00:54.310
We use singular they all the time

00:00:54.310 --> 00:00:56.440
when referring to an unknown person,

00:00:56.440 --> 00:00:58.770
such as in "Does anyone
know what they want?"

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:01.290
Or, "Each student took
their books home with them,"

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:04.300
or if you're BBC avoiding
MasterChef spoilers.

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:07.433
However, we know that language
does change over time.

00:01:07.433 --> 00:01:11.490
And today singular they can
also refer to a known person

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:14.507
who chooses it as their
self-identified pronoun.

00:01:14.507 --> 00:01:17.770
They was even chosen to be
the American Dialect Society's

00:01:17.770 --> 00:01:20.170
word of the year in 2015.

00:01:20.170 --> 00:01:22.658
But that's just scratching the
personal pronoun's surface.

00:01:22.658 --> 00:01:24.470
I'm Dr. Erica Brozovsky,

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:25.939
my personal pronouns are she/her,

00:01:25.939 --> 00:01:27.802
and this is OtherWords.

00:01:27.802 --> 00:01:30.552
(cheerful music)

00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:36.152
- [Presenter] Other Words.

00:01:36.152 --> 00:01:38.162
(cheerful music continues)

00:01:38.162 --> 00:01:40.770
- Anyone who thinks pronouns can't change

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:42.578
must not be much of a history buff,

00:01:42.578 --> 00:01:44.090
because they forgot what happened

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:46.951
to second-person pronouns
in the 17th century.

00:01:46.951 --> 00:01:48.800
During the early modern period,

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:51.930
English had four different
second-person pronouns,

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:54.830
thou, thee, ye and you,

00:01:54.830 --> 00:01:56.657
which Shakespeare took full advantage of.

00:01:56.657 --> 00:01:59.920
Kind of like with the
Spanish tú and usted,

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:02.303
thee and thou were informal and familiar,

00:02:02.303 --> 00:02:04.329
while you showed more respect.

00:02:04.329 --> 00:02:07.007
So in "Romeo and Juliet," Tybalt says,

00:02:07.007 --> 00:02:09.027
"Romeo, the love I bear thee

00:02:09.027 --> 00:02:12.529
"can afford no better term
than this, thou art a villain."

00:02:12.529 --> 00:02:15.567
While in "Richard II,"
York addresses the king,

00:02:15.567 --> 00:02:18.586
"O my liege, pardon me if you please."

00:02:18.586 --> 00:02:22.188
But in the 17th century, the
and thou fell out of fashion

00:02:22.188 --> 00:02:25.724
and were replaced with
the catchall pronoun you.

00:02:25.724 --> 00:02:27.290
Of course, there was a fuss.

00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:29.013
People are wary of change.

00:02:29.013 --> 00:02:31.040
One guy in particular, George Fox,

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:32.460
the founder of Quakerism,

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:35.050
wrote a full-on book in 1660

00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:38.557
calling everyone who used
singular you a fool and an idiot.

00:02:38.557 --> 00:02:40.200
And who's a fool now, buddy?

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:42.040
I think it's you.

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:45.169
While nowadays you can
refer to one or more people,

00:02:45.169 --> 00:02:47.320
some regions do tend to distinguish

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:49.718
between singular you
and a different plural.

00:02:49.718 --> 00:02:53.420
The most widely used is y'all,
which is typically associated

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:55.161
with the Southern United States.

00:02:55.161 --> 00:02:57.822
Although also found in South
African Indian English,

00:02:57.822 --> 00:02:59.909
Maori English, and in parts of Canada.

00:02:59.909 --> 00:03:01.632
Other common regionalisms in the U.S.

00:03:01.632 --> 00:03:04.467
are youse guys, you-uns, and yinz.

00:03:04.467 --> 00:03:07.230
Outside of the us in Trinidad,
you might hear allyuh.

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:10.849
In Ireland, yiz, and in Barbados, wunna,

00:03:10.849 --> 00:03:12.241
and that's just English.

00:03:12.241 --> 00:03:14.434
Many other world languages differentiate

00:03:14.434 --> 00:03:17.230
not only between singular and plural you,

00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:20.318
but also between familiar and
respectful versions of you.

00:03:20.318 --> 00:03:22.421
And some cultures just drop their pronouns

00:03:22.421 --> 00:03:23.780
a lot of the time,

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:25.200
because the information is built

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:26.607
into the verbs in the sentence.

00:03:26.607 --> 00:03:28.476
When people think of personal pronouns,

00:03:28.476 --> 00:03:30.481
especially when referring to themselves,

00:03:30.481 --> 00:03:32.321
they tend to think of gender.

00:03:32.321 --> 00:03:35.850
They is the most common
gender neutral pronoun

00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:39.311
but there are even more, which
is where neopronouns come in,

00:03:39.311 --> 00:03:42.448
third-person pronouns
beyond he, she or they,

00:03:42.448 --> 00:03:46.350
which may be used regardless
of gender expression, like ze,

00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:49.170
which states back to at least 1864,

00:03:49.170 --> 00:03:51.114
and hir from the early 20th century,

00:03:51.114 --> 00:03:52.670
both of which are in fact entries

00:03:52.670 --> 00:03:54.286
in the Oxford English Dictionary.

00:03:54.286 --> 00:03:56.010
People have various reasons

00:03:56.010 --> 00:03:57.472
for choosing the pronouns that they do.

00:03:57.472 --> 00:04:00.089
And as members of society,
it's our job to respect that.

00:04:00.089 --> 00:04:03.405
Gender aside, personal pronouns
are an interesting bunch.

00:04:03.405 --> 00:04:07.010
Take the word we, for
example, two little letters,

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:08.990
but a whole variety of usages,

00:04:08.990 --> 00:04:12.283
like the royal we, or people
saying, "We're pregnant!"

00:04:12.283 --> 00:04:15.116
We can be used to claim group identity.

00:04:15.116 --> 00:04:16.737
Your favorite team does well,

00:04:16.737 --> 00:04:18.263
"Yeah, we made the playoffs!"

00:04:18.263 --> 00:04:21.380
But if it didn't go so well, "They lost."

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:24.191
If I say, "We should hang
out," I mean you and me.

00:04:24.191 --> 00:04:26.127
If I'm telling you a
story about my weekend,

00:04:26.127 --> 00:04:28.594
and I say, "We went scuba diving,"

00:04:28.594 --> 00:04:30.846
the we doesn't include you.

00:04:30.846 --> 00:04:33.346
If your waiter says,
"How are we doing today?"

00:04:33.346 --> 00:04:35.554
they really just mean you.

00:04:35.554 --> 00:04:38.530
Sometimes, we is used to
deflect responsibility.

00:04:38.530 --> 00:04:40.264
"We don't think it's a good fit."

00:04:40.264 --> 00:04:44.000
Really, "I'm the hiring
manager who isn't feeling it."

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:46.030
Pronouns are tiny words

00:04:46.030 --> 00:04:47.929
but they say a lot more than you think.

00:04:47.929 --> 00:04:50.010
In "The Secret Life of Pronouns,"

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:53.360
social psychologist James
W. Pennebaker theorizes

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:55.900
that the frequency with
which we use certain pronouns

00:04:55.900 --> 00:04:58.890
can point to how we're
feeling, who we identify with,

00:04:58.890 --> 00:05:00.148
and even might indicate

00:05:00.148 --> 00:05:02.031
whether or not we're telling the truth.

00:05:02.031 --> 00:05:05.851
He also found an interesting
pronoun power dynamic.

00:05:05.851 --> 00:05:08.347
People in positions of
authority tend to use

00:05:08.347 --> 00:05:12.101
we and you at higher rates
than their subordinates.

00:05:12.101 --> 00:05:14.030
If you're emailing your boss,

00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:16.590
you probably use more I's than they do.

00:05:16.590 --> 00:05:19.320
I took a look at my emails and checks out.

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:20.803
And now you're gonna
be thinking about that

00:05:20.803 --> 00:05:22.630
next time me write an email.

00:05:22.630 --> 00:05:24.911
Pronouns deserve more
credit than they get.

00:05:24.911 --> 00:05:26.760
Our brains tend to ignore them

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:28.917
in favor of the juicier nouns and verbs,

00:05:28.917 --> 00:05:32.010
but there's a lot going
on under the surface.

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:34.580
Pronouns say a lot about
you both loud and proud

00:05:34.580 --> 00:05:36.290
when you state your personal pronouns,

00:05:36.290 --> 00:05:37.960
and also subconsciously,

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:40.313
in the way you think and speak and write.

00:05:40.313 --> 00:05:43.550
We use pronouns every day
in almost every sentence,

00:05:43.550 --> 00:05:46.740
and it'll be incredibly awkward
to communicate without them.

00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:49.100
In fact, every sentence I've said

00:05:49.100 --> 00:05:51.711
has a pronoun in it, except one.

00:05:51.711 --> 00:05:53.560
Good luck finding it.

00:05:53.560 --> 00:05:54.393
Before you go,

00:05:54.393 --> 00:05:57.325
could a blind gamer beat
you in Mortal Combat?

00:05:57.325 --> 00:05:59.356
Have you ever heard of the gay rodeo?

00:05:59.356 --> 00:06:02.357
And why are these guys
putting underwear on goats?

00:06:02.357 --> 00:06:05.551
"Subcultured," a brand new
documentary series from PBS,

00:06:05.551 --> 00:06:08.299
answers those questions and so many more,

00:06:08.299 --> 00:06:10.100
exploring lesser known communities

00:06:10.100 --> 00:06:12.855
and folks that have had a
major impact on the mainstream.

00:06:12.855 --> 00:06:15.177
Check it out over on PBS
Voices, link in our description,

00:06:15.177 --> 00:06:17.423
and let them know that
Other Words sent you.

00:06:17.423 --> 00:06:20.590
(bright guitar music)

